    <h2>What the earlier planets establish</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/lab/studies/mercury-2026-11-12#02-23"
        >Mercury’s NASA-map comparison</a
      >
      found recognizable surface organization useful, but the composed mineral
      mosaic read better at game scale. The delivered surface uses the authored
      exemplar, quilted into a wrapped atlas; it is not the NASA map. Practice
      loads that material through <code>authored-materials.ts</code> and the
      shared spherical projection. Its radius is 27u, orbit 258u, travel 4u/s
      and independent turn 31.3 seconds. Earth’s explicit imagery direction does
      not inherit Mercury’s synthesis method.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/lab/studies/venus-2026-11-13#02-1"
        >Venus’s first opaque cloud patterns</a
      >
      read as gas giants. The eventual body uses broad faint cloud structure,
      amber colour and strong shaded depth. Lifting the whole shadow lost depth.
      The wider atmospheric arc improved small-view presence but remained
      provisional enlarged. These findings motivate inspecting Earth’s whole
      globe at both scales; they do not prescribe an amber palette, opaque
      clouds or Venus’s arc for Earth.
    </p>
    <p>
      Current delivery is visible in the
      <a href="/lab/entities/planet/practice">production Practice preview</a>:
      Venus replaces the middle gas planet, while Mercury and the outer stock
      rock remain. Venus has radius about 40.33u, orbit 482.13u, travel 2.954u/s
      and a 60-second cloud turn. Both planets use simulation time, shared
      Sun-facing lighting and world labels/orbit/gravity markings. The relevant
      integration is <code>simulation.ts</code>, <code>flight-frame.ts</code>,
      <code>FlightDeck.svelte</code>
      and the core body painters. Earth needs a source-image material and a
      night emission treatment; existing hard light alone does not supply city
      lights.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/lab/studies/venus-2026-11-13#03">The later Practice retuning</a>
      reduced Mercury’s mass from 1450 to 870 and recalculated Venus’s
      separately stored mass to 2844.850580940663. Their reaches are about
      131.91u and 238.53u at the unchanged cut-off; size and motion stayed
      unchanged.
      <code>packages/mode-a/src/lib/tuning.json</code>
      and the planets and gravity-field contracts agree. The older study
      comparisons retain the original masses. These are adopted tuning changes,
      not evidence here about human playtesting or proof of encounter quality.
      Earth’s radius must not silently determine its mass. Whether Earth
      replaces the outer rock or adds a body, and its safe orbital placement,
      remain delivery choices after the visual direction is established.
    </p>
    <h2>Source material and its limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/blue-marble-next-generation/base-map/"
          >NASA Blue Marble: Next Generation base maps</a
        >: a starting point for recognizable land and ocean. Prefer a surface
        map to a globe photograph with its terminator already baked in. Asset
        resolution, processing and credits remain to be selected.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/the-blue-marble-true-color-global-imagery-at-1km-resolution/"
          >NASA’s 2002 Blue Marble construction</a
        >: surface observations are composited over time, and its cloud image
        combines observations from several days. Such a composite is a rendering
        source, not one simultaneous photograph or a future weather prediction.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/earth-at-night/maps/"
          >NASA Black Marble flat maps</a
        >: observed 2016 night lights offer geographically grounded habitation.
        Their distribution is a historical reference, not a map of civilization
        in 2400. Surface and light maps must share geographic alignment; cloud
        coverage and daylight suppression of lights remain visual questions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg1/chapter/summary-for-policymakers/"
          >IPCC AR6 physical-science summary</a
        >: long-term sea-level outcomes depend on emissions and ice-sheet
        uncertainty. It does not supply a definitive 2100–2400 globe. Reduced
        ice, changed vegetation or altered coasts are possible authored
        directions to research if chosen, not selected outcomes. No numerical
        climate scenario is adopted here.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The first useful comparison is an unaltered, source-based Earth against
      any proposed future treatment, under identical light and scale. This can
      distinguish a legibility adjustment from a fictional change to geography.
      The study permits source imagery as Earth’s material; it does not change
      the game-wide procedural art principle. Natural bodies already have
      distinct construction methods under <code>docs/design.md</code>.
    </p>
    <h2>Atmosphere emphasis · 14 November 2026</h2>
    <p>
      The revised question and opening make atmospheric presence central rather
      than one item in a list of visual properties. Deliberate rendering is
      permitted independently of fictional climate changes: exaggerating haze or
      apparent thickness for legibility does not require inventing a different
      Earth. The intended quality is a substantial world seen through air, with
      weather and inhabited darkness, belonging beside Mercury and Venus. Exact
      treatment remains open. The question-test stamps above describe the
      earlier framing; this revision has not been independently rerun.
    </p>
